size the picture for this panel: 3x actual, height-filling stretch

Stretch mode stopped short of the screen edges. Upstream's canvas and its
picture are both 720x480, so "stretch" fills the panel by construction; 0002
made the canvas the 752x560 panel and deliberately left the picture at 720x480
("keeping it separate is what lets the canvas be the panel while the game stays
where it was"), and nothing taught stretch the difference -- it filled all but
a 16px border down each side and 40px top and bottom, on the mode whose whole
job is to fill the screen.

GAME_W and GAME_H cannot simply be raised to the panel: SRC_W and SRC_H derive
from them, so that would resize the 240x160 source texture. So the picture's
size on the canvas is a size the frontend chooses, and screen_rect takes it.
That half is in 0002, which owns power.rs, pipeline.rs and soft/mod.rs; this is
the half that chooses.

Sizes are multiples of the whole 240x160 texture rather than of the 160x144
picture inside it, which is what makes this cheap: the paper around the picture
scales with it, the lcd3x grid stays aligned to source pixels, the canvas clips
the overhang -- paper first -- and `pic` keeps gating the grid exactly as
before.

That also means source_rect must stop cropping. Upstream cropped to the
platform's picture when stretching and drew the crop across a canvas-sized
destination; doing that *and* sizing the destination from the texture
multiplies the two. It shipped that way for one build and a stretched Game Boy
came out at 5.83x by 4.32x -- a 933x622 picture on a 752x560 panel, wildly
zoomed. The source rect is now the whole texture always, and
the_effective_scale_is_never_doubled_up checks the product of the two rather
than either alone, which is the mistake that let it through: each was right on
its own.

On this 752x560 panel:

  actual    3x, 480x432, 136px of paper a side. Pixel-exact, unchanged, and the
            only size where a source pixel is one square block.
  stretch   3.889x for GB/GBC, filling the height: 622x560, 65px of paper a
            side, nothing cropped, aspect right.
  GBA       unchanged in both modes. source_rect already returns WHOLE_TEXTURE
            for it either way, so stretch is a no-op upstream, and the largest
            aspect-correct fit here is 3.133x -- non-integer pixels for 4% more
            picture, a bad trade nobody asked for.

Two other sizes were built, looked at on the handheld, and rejected: the whole
752x560, which is 21% wider than square pixels, and a whole 4x at 640x560,
which is uniform and 2.9% bigger than the above but crops 2 source rows off the
top and 2 off the bottom. The crop was the part that was disliked, which is the
sort of thing only the panel can tell you -- the arithmetic preferred 4x.

Upstream's own test for this, l_fills_the_panel_and_r_gives_back_the_centred
_picture, could not catch the original bug: it is macOS-only, and its 120px bar
is narrower than this panel's 136px actual-size margin, so it passes either way.
The tests here pin the sizes that reach the panel instead.

app.rs is shared with 0006, in a different place.

